Module position ALONG ITS OWN AXIS - `slide`, the degree of freedom a revolute joint leaves free (EXO-170, generalised by EXO-214). SOLVED, never an input, no per-joint override. The axis LINE is untouched, so the kinematics stay the URDF's exactly.
  EXO-214: a slide is the MINIMUM along the module's own axis that makes the whole pair FIT - not just the minimum that opens the two bodies. Fitting is three things, each of them a rule that already existed: the module BODIES clear (armgen.MODULE_PROFILE, the real solid of revolution EXO-166 measured, not an infinite cylinder); the LINK between them routes, sized with that joint's own link stop; and whatever pad chord EXO-181 still needs MISSES that stop block by STOP_CHORD_MARGIN 1.0 mm, with the link's rotor join clearing module B by the same target the bodies are asked for. The cost is paid one for one as run by the link that lands on the slid module.
  target gap 2.00 mm = MIN_CLEARANCE 1.0 + SLIDE_MARGIN 1.0; scanned at 2.0 mm and bisected to 1e-03 mm; module surface sampled 120 x 71
