Which LANDING primitive the pair gets (EXO-176) and where the level change is taken inside it (EXO-158). The landing plate is a flat disk, so its plane carries two free directions; `square` is EXO-144's, with the tongue pinned to the rotor pad axis, and `angled` aims the tongue FROM THE DISK CENTRE AT THE POINT WHERE THE RIBBON ENTERS THE PLANE, so the pair may spend its lateral offset inside the plate plane, where it costs no straight run. Both are sized in full, square then angled first, and the first feasible one is taken - so a pair the square landing can take keeps exactly the route it had.
